<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What may leave this device, and by which route (Android 12+).

  Everything the app persists (presets, history, profile, the daily quota and
  the signed-in session) shares one SharedPreferences file, CapacitorStorage,
  because that is the single store the Preferences plugin writes to. The
  session inside it holds a refresh token, and Preferences.configure({ group })
  is global and asynchronous, so the token cannot be split into a file of its
  own without racing every other read.

  The two backup routes are treated differently instead, because they are not
  the same risk.

  cloud-backup copies to Google's servers. Bearer credentials should not go
  there, from an app whose whole promise is that nothing leaves the phone, so
  the preferences are excluded.

  device-transfer is a direct phone to phone copy during setup, with no third
  party holding anything. Presets someone built for their exam forms are worth
  carrying across an upgrade, so that route keeps them.

  The cost, stated plainly: restoring from a cloud backup starts signed out and
  without saved presets. Restoring by device transfer keeps both.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
  <cloud-backup>
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="CapacitorStorage.xml" />
  </cloud-backup>

  <device-transfer>
    <!-- Everything, including the preferences excluded from cloud backup. -->
  </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
